(Description)
- You are given an array of k linked-lists lists, each linked-list is sorted in ascending order.
- Merge all the linked-lists into one sorted linked-list and return it.

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
        return merge(lists, null, null);
    }

    private ListNode merge(ListNode[] lists, ListNode result, ListNode lastNode) {
        int minIndex = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
            if (lists[i] != null && (minIndex == -1 || lists[i].val < lists[minIndex].val)) {
                minIndex = i;
            }
        }

        if (minIndex == -1) {
            return result;
        }

        ListNode current = lists[minIndex];
        lists[minIndex] = lists[minIndex].next;

        if (result == null) {
            result = current;
            lastNode = current;
        }
        else {
            lastNode.next = current;
            lastNode = current;
        }

        return merge(lists, result, lastNode);
    }
}

--- Approach ---
In the recursion, it finds the right node involving smallest value throughout using for loop statement.
It keeps the minIndex, and keep updating the index if a node, that haves smaller value, was found in the loop.

Therefore, base case is when minIndex was not changed, meaning all of the nodes were sorted already.
Or, it moves the ListNode to the next node if the smallest node was added.

In the result ListNode, because it should add to the last node, recursion pass two nodes result(first node to return) & lastnode(appending easiily to the last)
